  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209228/assets/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$",
